#### Problem 2: Lack of FHIR-Specific Permission Scopes

OAuth 2.0 scopes are typically coarse-grained application permissions (e.g., `read_user_data`, `write_content`). Healthcare requires fine-grained permissions at the FHIR resource type level to implement least-privilege access control and satisfy HIPAA minimum necessary standard.

**Real-World Manifestation:**

A member uses a third-party medication adherence app that needs to read medication history and set reminders. With generic OAuth scopes:

- App must request broad `read_all_health_data` scope (no granular alternative)
- Member cannot consent to "medications only" — must grant access to all health data including sensitive mental health records, HIV status, substance abuse treatment
- Member abandons app registration due to excessive permissions requested (privacy concern)

**Technical Gap:**

```typescript
// Generic OAuth scopes are too broad for healthcare
const scopes = [
  'read_health_data',    // Grants access to ALL FHIR resources (excessive)
  'write_health_data'    // Grants ability to create/modify ANY resource (dangerous)
];
// No way to express: "read medications only" or "read observations and conditions only"
```

**Impact:**
- Security teams are concerned that excessive permissions create a large blast radius if an app is compromised; overly broad scope requests may result in integration rejections.
- HIPAA §164.514(d)(3) minimum necessary standard violation: apps accessing more PHI than needed for their function

**SMART Solution:**

SMART defines granular FHIR resource-type scopes with read/write separation and patient/user/system context:

```typescript
// SMART scopes enable granular FHIR resource permissions
const scopes = [
  'patient/MedicationRequest.read',       // Read member's prescriptions only
  'patient/MedicationStatement.read',     // Read member's reported medications only
  'patient/Observation.read',             // Read lab values and vitals (for drug interactions)
  // Scope pattern: {context}/{ResourceType}.{read|write|*}
  // Context: patient (patient's own data), user (clinician role-based), system (backend bulk)
];

// Member sees granular consent screen:
// "Medication Adherence App requests permission to:"
//  ✓ Read your prescriptions (MedicationRequest)
//  ✓ Read your medications (MedicationStatement)
//  ✓ Read your lab results (Observation)
//
// "This app will NOT have access to:"
//  ✗ Your diagnoses (Condition)
//  ✗ Your medical procedures (Procedure)
//  ✗ Your clinical notes (DocumentReference)
```

