#### Problem 3: No Standardized Bulk Data Access for Backend Services

Payer-to-payer data exchange and population health analytics require bulk FHIR data export (thousands or millions of resources). Interactive OAuth authorization flows designed for user-facing apps don't work for backend batch processes running overnight without user interaction.

**Real-World Manifestation:**

New York State Medicaid requires Gainwell to implement payer-to-payer data exchange. When a member switches from Gainwell-managed plan to competitor plan:

1. Member consents to data sharing via competitor's member portal
2. Competitor's backend system needs to bulk-export member's health history (5 years of claims, clinical data = 12,000+ FHIR resources)
3. Export must complete within 24 hours per CMS requirements
4. **Generic OAuth requires user interaction**: Competitor would need to redirect member to Gainwell authorization page every time they run batch export
5. Batch job runs at 2 AM when members are asleep — no one available to click "authorize" button

**Technical Gap:**

```typescript
// OAuth Authorization Code Flow requires user interaction (doesn't work for backend services)
async function bulkExportMemberData(memberId: string) {
  // Step 1: Redirect user to authorization page (IMPOSSIBLE for backend batch job)
  window.location.href = `https://oauth.gainwell.com/authorize?...`;

  // Step 2: User approves (IMPOSSIBLE at 2 AM)

  // Step 3: Get authorization code from redirect (IMPOSSIBLE - no browser)
  const code = getCodeFromRedirectURL();

  // Backend batch process CANNOT complete this flow
}
```

**Impact:**
- New York State Medicaid escalated non-compliance issue to Gainwell executive leadership (July 2024)

**SMART Solution:**

SMART Backend Services authorization uses OAuth 2.0 Client Credentials Flow with asymmetric key authentication (JWT assertion) for automated backend access:

```typescript
// SMART Backend Services: No user interaction required
async function bulkExportMemberData(memberId: string) {
  // Step 1: Create JWT assertion signed with private key
  const jwt = createJWTAssertion({
    iss: 'competitor-payer-client-id',    // Client ID
    sub: 'competitor-payer-client-id',    // Same as issuer
    aud: 'https://oauth.gainwell.com/token',
    exp: Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000) + 300,  // 5-minute expiration
    jti: generateUniqueID()
  }, privateKey);  // Signed with private key (registered with Gainwell)

  // Step 2: Exchange JWT for access token (no user interaction)
  const tokenResponse = await fetch('https://oauth.gainwell.com/token', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: {'Content-Type': 'application/x-www-form-urlencoded'},
    body: new URLSearchParams({
      grant_type: 'client_credentials',
      client_assertion_type: 'urn:ietf:params:oauth:client-assertion-type:jwt-bearer',
      client_assertion: jwt,
      scope: 'system/*.read'  // System-level scope for backend access
    })
  });

  const {access_token} = await tokenResponse.json();

  // Step 3: Initiate FHIR bulk export (runs asynchronously)
  const exportResponse = await fetch('https://fhir.gainwell.com/Patient/$export', {
    method: 'GET',
    headers: {
      'Authorization': `Bearer ${access_token}`,
      'Accept': 'application/fhir+json',
      'Prefer': 'respond-async'
    }
  });
  // Returns 202 Accepted with Content-Location header for status polling
  // Backend job polls status endpoint until export completes (typically 10-30 minutes for large datasets)
}
```

